tion claims k=0,1,3 are possible, and k=2 is impossible, and k≥4 impossible.\n\nWe need to verify if this is correct. Let's analyze the problem ourselves to confirm.\n\nFirst, we need to understand the constraints. The set T_n = {(a,b) ∈ ℤ^2: a,b ≥ 1, a+b ≤ n+1}. There are N = n(n+1)/2 points.\n\nWe need n distinct lines covering all points. So we have exactly n lines, each line can cover multiple points. The lines can be any lines (including vertical, horizontal, slope -1, or any other slope). Exactly k of them must be sunny (i.e., slope not 0, ∞, -1). So the other n-k lines are non-sunny: they are either horizontal (slope 0), vertical (slope ∞), or slope -1.\n\nWe need to find all possible k for which such a configuration exists.\n\nThe solution's reasoning: they argue that a sunny line can meet each column (i.e., each fixed x coordinate) at most one point, because if it meets two points in same column, then line would be vertical (non-sunny). That's correct: a line with two points having same x coordinate is vertical, slope ∞, not sunny. So each sunny line can cover at most one point per column.\n\nSimilarly, a non-sunny line can be vertical (covers all points in a column), horizontal (covers at most one point per column, but also at most one per row), or slope -1 (covers at most one per column as well, because slope -1 lines are not vertical). Actually slope -1 lines can intersect a column at exactly one point (since they are not vertical). So only vertical lines can cover multiple points in a column. Horizontal lines can cover multiple points in a row (i.e., same y coordinate). But the points are arranged in a triangular shape; each row also has decreasing number of points.\n\nThus to cover columns with many points, we need vertical lines.\n\nThe solution uses the three rightmost columns: column x=n has 1 point (n,1); column x=n-1 has 2 points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2); column x=n-2 has 3 points (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). They argue that if k ≤ 2, then column n-2 (size 3) cannot be covered solely by sunny lines because each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so need at least 3 sunny lines to cover all three points, but we have at most 2 sunny lines. So at least one non-sunny line must cover some points in column n-2. Since only vertical lines can cover multiple points in a column, they claim that a vertical line x=n-2 must be present. That seems plausible.\n\nThen they argue about column n-1: if we don't use vertical line x=n-1, then both points in column n-1 must be covered by sunny lines, each covering exactly one point. Then they consider point (n,1) in column n. They claim that a line joining (n,1) with either of the two points of column n-1 is either horizontal (if (n-1,1) is used) or slope -1 (if (n-1,2) is used). Indeed, line through (n,1) and (n-1,1) is horizontal (slope 0). Line through (n,1) and (n-1,2) has slope (2-1)/(n-1-n) = 1/(-1) = -1. So both are non-sunny. So (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with a point from column n-1. So (n,1) would need a third sunny line (if we try to cover it with a sunny line that also covers some other point). But we only have k ≤ 2 sunny lines. So contradiction. So k=2 impossible.\n\nNow they argue that k≥4 also impossible: \"Since a sunny line can meet each column in at most one point, having k≥4 would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points, leaving at most n-4 vertical lines to cover the remaining columns. Because the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2, we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered.\" This argument is a bit vague. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe have n lines total. Suppose k≥4. Then we have n-k non-sunny lines, which can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. Horizontal and slope -1 lines cannot cover more than one point per column, so they are not helpful for covering columns with many points. So to cover columns with many