n is a vertical line (since horizontal lines cover multiple points in a row, not column; slope -1 lines cover multiple points with same sum, which are not in same column). So to cover a column with more than k points, we need a vertical line at that column.\n\nThus, for each column c, if h_c > k, we need a vertical line x=c among the non-sunny lines. Since we have n-k non-sunny lines total, we can have at most n-k vertical lines (some may be horizontal or slope -1). So we need to ensure that the number of columns with h_c > k is ≤ n-k.\n\nNow h_c = n+1-c. So columns are decreasing from n to 1. For a given k, columns with h_c > k are those with n+1-c > k => c < n+1 - k. So columns c = 1,2,..., n - k. That's n - k columns. So we need at most n - k vertical lines to cover those columns. But we have exactly n - k non-sunny lines, which could be all vertical lines. So it's possible to assign vertical lines to each of those columns. However, we also need to cover the remaining columns (c = n - k + 1, ..., n) which have h_c ≤ k. For those columns, we could cover each point with a sunny line (one per point) or with horizontal or slope -1 lines. But we have only k sunny lines total, not per column. So we need to allocate sunny lines across columns.\n\nLet's think: For columns with h_c ≤ k, each column has at most k points, but we have only k sunny lines total, each can cover at most one point per column. So across all those columns, we need to cover all points using at most k sunny lines plus possibly some non-sunny lines (horizontal or slope -1). But we have used all n - k non-sunny lines as vertical lines for the first n - k columns. So we have no remaining non-sunny lines to cover points in the remaining columns. So the remaining columns must be covered entirely by sunny lines. But we have only k sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column. So across the remaining columns (which are k columns: c = n - k + 1 to n), each column has at most k points, but we need